<section class="weekly-section weekly-section--lead">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">Opening finding</div>
<h2>Cerbos describes human approval bound to one payment amount and destination</h2>
<p class="weekly-lead-p">Cerbos's 29 October explanation of its iProov integration says high-risk actions are held for a named approver, with a signed, single-use credential tied to the action. Changing the payment amount or destination blocks the action, according to Cerbos.<sup><a aria-label="Source 1" href="#s1">1</a></sup></p>
<p>This specifies more than human participation: approval must match what executes. It is vendor-described behavior, not an independently verified enterprise deployment; the underlying service launched on 22 October.</p>
<div class="weekly-figure"><div class="weekly-figcaption-label">Explanatory diagram / Vendor-described control</div><div class="weekly-flow weekly-flow--3"><div class="weekly-flow-step"><span class="weekly-flow-num">01 / ENTERPRISE RULE</span><b>Hold the payment</b><span>Policy requires a named person's approval.</span></div><div class="weekly-flow-step"><span class="weekly-flow-num">02 / ACTION-BOUND APPROVAL</span><b>Amount + destination</b><span>Human presence check. Signed credential. One use.</span></div><div class="weekly-flow-step weekly-flow-step--outcome"><span class="weekly-flow-num">03 / MISMATCH</span><b>Changed payment blocked</b><span>A changed amount or destination no longer matches.</span></div></div><div class="weekly-limit">Not a customer system or a reproduced test. The final payment processor's rejection path remains unverified.</div><p class="weekly-figcaption"><span class="weekly-figcaption-label">Source and credit</span>Jochanni Labs explanatory visual, based on <a href="https://www.cerbos.dev/blog/human-in-the-loop-approval-for-ai-agents-cerbos-iproov" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Cerbos, 29 October 2026</a>. It depicts the published mechanism, not production evidence.</p></div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">01 / Market Response</div>
<h2>Control descriptions are not deployment evidence</h2>
<div class="weekly-table-wrap"><table class="weekly-table"><thead><tr><th>Organization</th><th>Reported response</th><th>Evidence level</th><th>Unverified</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cerbos / iProov<sup><a aria-label="Source 1" href="#s1">1</a></sup></td><td>Hold high-risk actions; bind one approval to one action.</td><td>Partner explanation, 29 Oct</td><td>Customer deployment; final executor checks.</td></tr><tr><td>NodeFlare<sup><a aria-label="Source 2" href="#s2">2</a></sup></td><td>Publish a server-side confirmation gate with negative tests.</td><td>Code and author-reported tests, 28 Oct</td><td>Human presence; production deletion.</td></tr><tr><td>OpenRouter<sup><a aria-label="Source 3" href="#s3">3</a></sup></td><td>Remove disallowed email tool calls before application execution.</td><td>Vendor benchmark, 29 Oct</td><td>Deterministic authority; mail-system rejection.</td></tr><tr><td>NIST<sup><a aria-label="Source 5" href="#s5">5</a></sup></td><td>Propose per-call verification and risk-triggered human review.</td><td>Design-tenet blog, 27 Oct</td><td>Implementation results; not a binding standard.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>These sources document market response, not enterprise adoption counts. NIST separates policy from agent reasoning and favors deterministic access decisions.</p>
</div>
