<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">02 / Incidents and Enforcement Gaps</div>
<h2>New test disclosures, not confirmed production incidents</h2>
<p>In this reviewed case set: two demonstrated vulnerabilities and one blocked-attempt benchmark. No confirmed unauthorized production execution or qualifying incident update was established. These are source-set counts, not an incident rate.</p>
<p><strong>Demonstrated vulnerability, Datadog email test:</strong> an agent copied injected instructions into an HTTP reply to a test collector. Blocking was disabled; no real inbox received it.<sup><a aria-label="Source 4" href="#s4">4</a></sup></p>
<p><strong>Demonstrated vulnerability, Datadog CSV test:</strong> an agent copied a fake warning into a skill file and issued a customer-file deletion command. The shell was simulated, so no host files changed. AI Guard marked the skill write unsafe but the deletion command safe.<sup><a aria-label="Source 4" href="#s4">4</a></sup></p>
<p><strong>Blocked attempt, OpenRouter benchmark:</strong> without Armor, the reported configurations produced 73 unauthorized email tool calls; the two Armor configurations produced zero across 1,440 attack trials. These are laboratory tool-call outcomes, not evidence of delivered mail.<sup><a aria-label="Source 3" href="#s3">3</a></sup></p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">03 / Domains Requiring Enforcement</div>
<h2>Check the action where it takes effect</h2>
<div class="weekly-table-wrap"><table class="weekly-table"><thead><tr><th>Domain / source</th><th>Exact action</th><th>Authority check belongs</th><th>Consequence if absent</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Payments<sup><a aria-label="Source 1" href="#s1">1</a></sup></td><td>Send a payment</td><td>Payment execution boundary, binding amount and destination</td><td>Unapproved transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>Messaging<sup><a aria-label="Source 3" href="#s3">3</a></sup><sup><a aria-label="Source 4" href="#s4">4</a></sup></td><td>Send email or an HTTP reply</td><td>Send handler, binding recipient and body</td><td>Unauthorized disclosure or copied injection.</td></tr><tr><td>Agent workspace<sup><a aria-label="Source 2" href="#s2">2</a></sup><sup><a aria-label="Source 4" href="#s4">4</a></sup></td><td>Delete a project/file; write a skill</td><td>Deletion and file-write handlers, binding target and content</td><td>Data loss or persistent injected instructions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">04 / Enforcement Evidence and DAL Impact</div>
<h2>Exact arguments matter. Reviewer authority still needs proof.</h2>
<p>NodeFlare's 28 October sample checks verified user identity, tool, exact-argument hash, expiry and a single-use nonce. Its author reports rejection of changed arguments, cross-user replay and reused approval. The sample returns deletion text rather than calling a deletion API; an auto-accepting client still passes. It does not prove a human approved or held the required organizational authority.<sup><a aria-label="Source 2" href="#s2">2</a></sup></p>
<p>OpenRouter uses a decision model, not deterministic authorization. Its Chat Completions blocked-call markers exposed the synthetic secret in 19 of 73 blocked calls, a separate output-handling defect.<sup><a aria-label="Source 3" href="#s3">3</a></sup></p>
<p>Decision Governance is the category. The Decision Authority Layer is the runtime enforcement layer; DAL-X is the application Jochanni Labs is building. System access does not establish authority to execute a specific action. Enterprise trigger logic identifies actions requiring an authority decision.</p>
<p><strong>Conditional assessment:</strong> DAL-style enforcement could stop an injected customer-file deletion only if its trigger covers that deletion, the reviewer has valid authority, approval binds the exact payload, and the downstream handler rejects missing, changed, expired or reused authority across every relevant execution path, including direct shell access. It would not establish that an approved deletion was correct.<sup><a aria-label="Source 6" href="#s6">6</a></sup></p>
<p class="weekly-status">DAL-X status: sandbox work, not a customer deployment. One public site reports end-to-end real-system sandbox execution; the other still describes simulation with production controls incomplete. No enterprise pilot or production enforcement is established by these pages.<sup><a aria-label="Source 7" href="#s7">7</a></sup><sup><a aria-label="Source 8" href="#s8">8</a></sup></p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="weekly-section">
<div class="container">
<div class="weekly-secnum">05 / Enterprise Check and Next Week's Watch</div>
<h2>Test changed actions and bypass paths</h2>
<div class="weekly-check"><p><strong>Payment:</strong> change the destination after approval and replay the credential. Effective control requires rejection at the executor, no transfer, and matching authority and outcome records.</p>
<p><strong>File deletion:</strong> attempt the same deletion through the tool and direct shell. Effective control requires both paths to reject absent authority and leave the file unchanged.</p></div>
<p>Watch for Cerbos executor-level tests and customer evidence. Track NodeFlare's human-presence limitation and OpenRouter's blocked-output handling. These remain unresolved, not forecast outcomes.</p>
<div class="weekly-question">Can your downstream system reject the exact action your reviewer did not authorize?</div>
